=begin

Mixin = only a collection of methods (a bunch of method definitions).
Not a class, mixin cann't be instantiated

In Ruby a class has 1 superclass (inherits its methods), but also can include
number of mixins (gets their methods too). Like adding method definitions to
some class, but avoids copy/paste them when including a mixin.
Include mixin makes its methods part of the class:
- extend or override in the order mixins are included. E.g. method m in
mixin 1 overriden by m in mixin 2 included later
- can use self->  more powerful than helper methods because mixin methods can
access methods and instance variables on self not defined in the mixin, become
part of class definition

Mixins change method lookup:
- when looking for message receiver obj's method m, look in obj's class, then
mixins that class includes (check mixins in order where a later include will
shadow an earlier include), then obj's superclass, then the superclass's mixins
etc. 
- mixin methods can get/set instance variables (since included in same object),
but poor style for mixin methods to access instance variables

=end

# mixin Double definition using module keyword
# module supports also namespace management
module Doubler
  def double
    # uses self's + message, not defined in Doubler, assumes any class that
    # includes this mixin, will have + defined
    self + self
  end
end

class Pt
  attr_accessor :x, :y
  include Doubler #include mixin so class Pt has double method 
  def + other
    ans = Pt.new
    ans.x = self.x + other.x
    ans.y = self.y + other.y
    ans
  end
end

# update built-in class String to add method double to concatenate s to itself
class String
  include Doubler
end

=begin
Popular mixins

Comparable
defines methods <, >, ==, !=, >=, <= in terms of <=> (spaceship operator)
assumed to be defined by class that includes this mixin.
<=> returns negative number if left operand is less (or if current object is
less than argument passed to method <=>), 0 if they're equal and positive number
if right operand is less

> 3 <=> 7
=> -1 
> 3 <=> 3
=> 0 
> 4 <=> 3
=> 1
Like in sequence of 3 symbols <=> with index -1 for <, 0 for = and 1 for >
since 3 less than 4 we produce index corresponding to < which is -1
3 < 2 (=> false) is implemented by Comparable mixin included by class Integer
defining < to call <=> then check if result is < 0  

Enumerable
defines many iterators (e.g. map, find) in terms of each. Need to define in
class including Enumerable a method each.

No need for multiple inheritance with Ruby's mixins: a class can inherit
specific methods from its superclass and include mixins to add more methods.
Mixins though are not full replacement for multiple inheritance because
Color can be a mixin for Point (adds extra functionality), but Artist and
Cowbow need to be equal subclasses of Person and it would be odd for an
ArtistCowboy to just inherit one subclass Artist and include mixin CowboyMethods
and still ArtistCowboy doesn't get me two pockets.

=end

# these are probably the two most common uses in the Ruby library:
#  Comparable and Enumerable

# you define <=> and you get ==, >, <, >=, <= from the mixin
# (overrides Object's ==, adds the others)
class Name
  attr_accessor :first, :middle, :last
  include Comparable #defines  ==, >, <, >=, <=, !=
  def initialize(first,last,middle="")
    @first = first
    @last = last
    @middle = middle
  end

  # define spaceship operator 
  def <=> other
    l = @last <=> other.last # <=> defined on strings
    #if not same last name return l, else keep comparing
    return l if l != 0
    f = @first <=> other.first
    #if same last name, not same first name return f, else keep comparing
    return f if f != 0
    @middle <=> other.middle
  end
end

=begin
> n1 = Name.new("Daniel","Grossman", "Joseph")
=> #<Name:0x4387b79e @middle="Joseph", @first="Daniel", @last="Grossman"> 
> n2 = Name.new("Barrack","Obama", "Hussein")
=> #<Name:0x2cbb3d47 @middle="Hussein", @first="Barrack", @last="Obama"> 
> n1 <=> n2
=> -1 
> n1 > n2
=> false 
> n1 != n2
=> true
=end

# Note ranges are built in and very common, to avoid conflict name MyRange
# you define each and you get map, any?, etc.
# (note map returns an array though)
# mixin Enumerable doesn't know how to iterate through a MyRange, just calls
# each and passes it the block, and each creates loop calling block from low
# upto high

class MyRange
  include Enumerable
  def initialize(low,high)
    @low = low
    @high = high
  end
  def each
    # for low, upto high, using a while loop it yields (calls block with i)
    # then increments i
    # local variable i starts as @low
    i=@low
    while i <= @high
      yield i #call block with current i as argument
      i=i+1
    end
  end
end

=begin
> r2 = MyRange.new(5,12)
=> #<MyRange:0x13c10b87 @high=12, @low=5> 
> r2.each {|i| puts i}
5
6
7
8
9
10
11
12
=> nil 
> r2.count {|i| i.odd?}
=> 4 
> r2.any?
=> true
=end

